# Line endings, for the files where they are load-bearing.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites
# checked-out files to CRLF. For source that is only ever read by a compiler
# that is harmless. For a shell script that becomes the entrypoint of a Linux
# container it is fatal and the error does not say so: the kernel reads the
# shebang literally, finds `/bin/sh\r`, and reports
#
#   exec /usr/local/bin/entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# which sends you looking for a missing file that is plainly present. The
# signer's entrypoint is what drops privilege inside the enclave, so this is
# the difference between a TEE that boots and one that does not.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `text=auto`: the first is an instruction, the second is
# a guess about content that a CRLF working tree can still win.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning, one step removed. Neither is a shebang, but both are consumed
# by Linux tooling that treats a trailing CR as part of the value — a
# `SERVICE_TOKEN=…\r` reaching the enclave as a secret would fail the bearer
# comparison against a token that looks identical in every log.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
compose.yaml text eol=lf
